# TypeScript Standards

## Canonical Messaging Types
```typescript
export interface Message {
  channelId: string;
  content: string;
  userId: string;
  userEmail: string;
  userName: string;
  organizationId: string;
  timestamp: string;
  recipientId?: string;
  messageId?: string;
  tempId?: string;
  metadata?: {
    type: 'text' | 'file' | 'image';
    timestamp?: string;
  };
}

export interface Channel {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  type: 'channel' | 'direct';
  unreadCount?: number;
  lastMessage?: string;
  lastMessageAt?: string;
  members?: string[];
}

type ProviderId = 'unified' | 'gmail' | 'whatsapp' | 'internal';
```
- Reuse and extend the definitions in `src/widgets/frontdesk/providers/internal/types/internal.types.ts` instead of re-declaring new shapes.
- Add provider-specific fields via intersection types or dedicated interfaces (e.g., `EmailConversation extends ConversationBase`).

## Component Contracts
```typescript
interface ConversationListProps {
  conversations: Channel[];
  selectedId?: string;
  onSelect: (conversation: Channel) => void;
  loading?: boolean;
  hasMore?: boolean;
}

export const ConversationList: React.FC<ConversationListProps> = ({
  conversations,
  selectedId,
  onSelect,
  loading = false,
}) => {
  // ...
};
```
- Always declare prop interfaces/types; default to `React.FC<Props>` for provider-level components.
- Prefer discriminated unions for multi-state models (e.g., message status, presence states).

## Strictness Guidelines
- Avoid `any`; if a third-party payload is unknown, type it as `unknown` and refine with user-defined type guards.
- Explicitly type async function return values and hook outputs.
- Extract literal unions/enums for fixed values (`type ConnectionStatus = 'connecting' | 'connected' | 'disconnected' | 'error';`).
- Share reusable types under provider `types/` directories or `src/interfaces/frontdesk/` if they cross providers.

## Utility Types
- Use `Partial<T>` for patch operations, `Pick<T, K>` for selection, and generics for reusable helpers (e.g., pagination responses).
- Mirror backend DTO names when possible so API integration stays intuitive.
